  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{8{n}^{6}+4{n}^{5}}{8{n}^{4}+10}$ for $n\\ge 1?$",
  "worked_solution": "The dominant term in the denominator is $8{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{6}+4{n}^{5}}{8{n}^{4}+10} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8{n}^{6}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{4{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{8{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{10}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{2}+4n}{(8+\\frac{10}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{2}+4n}{8+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }({n}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}n) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.",
